#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060792} :activity/id #uuid "ecbbd6ab-a6c2-56dd-b370-f0b0a63b7495" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049532 :topic/id #uuid "fc9eb3d6-7778-5857-a254-5fdcc6293315" :topic/math-academy-id 269} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060792 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060905 :tutorial/content "A **quadrantal angle** is an angle whose terminal side lies on the $x$ or $y$-axis.\nThe unit circle below illustrates the quadrantal angles that lie on the range $[0^\\circ, 360^\\circ)$.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nWe can identify four points on the unit circle above,\n\n$$\n(1,0), \\quad (0,1), \\quad (-1,0), \\quad (0,-1)\n$$\n\nthat correspond to the angles\n\n$$\n0^\\circ, \\quad 90^\\circ,\\quad 180^\\circ, \\quad 270^\\circ\n$$\n\nin degrees, or\n\n$$\n0, \\quad \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\quad \\pi, \\quad \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nin radians.\n\nWe can use these points to find cosine and sine of quadrantal angles. First, recall that a point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nFor example, if we are asked to find the value of $\\cos(\\pi)$, then we just look at the $\\color{blue}x$-coordinate of the corresponding point. The point is $({\\color{blue}{-1}},0)$, and therefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\cos(\\pi)={\\color{blue}{-1}}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0a9cc81f-6c96-5bec-b790-4065bdc2dd14" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2723 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9d6b7ce1-f570-53ff-9cbe-4697b39df083" :step/math-academy-id 453667 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060793}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060793 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059840 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059515 :question/id #uuid "e8b9ae0a-cc32-558d-9b59-f7c7608a4eaa" :question/math-academy-id "e-3863"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b406